#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.10"
# dependencies = [
#     "pykeepass>=4.1,<5",
# ]
# ///
"""merge-kdbx - join several KeePass (KDBX) databases into one new database.

The first input file is copied 1:1 into the output.  Every following input is
merged into the result in command line order:

  * groups are matched by name along the whole path; missing groups are created
  * entries that do not exist yet (same group + same title) are copied as-is
  * entries whose title already exists in the target group are copied with an
    indexed title ("item - 1", "item - 2", ...) and get a
    "source: <input path>" line appended to their notes
  * identical attachments are stored once; every entry carrying such an
    attachment gets a note listing all input files it was found in

Nothing is ever overwritten and input files are only ever opened read-only.
All entry data is copied at XML level, so every property (times, expiry,
custom fields, attachments, history, icons, tags, auto-type, ...) is kept.

Every input except the first is decrypted in a short-lived child process.
Its master password, derived keys and decrypted database die with that
process; the parent only receives the entries that go into the output.

Example:
    merge-kdbx -i file1.kdbx some/path/file2.kdbx -ip user -o merged.kdbx -p secret
"""

